From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Mon, 22 Aug 1994 10:58:38 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-245] Does existence exist?

Steve Witham writes:
>>But there is the common, and it seems to me self-consistent, view that
>>it just isn't like anything inside inside a universe that doesn't exist.
>
>I think it's maybe self-consistent, but adds nothing.  That is, it doesn't
>help explain or find out about anything to say that a universe does or
>doesn't exist.

If "existence doesn't exist", and if two different things having
experiences with the same content are deemed to be having the very *same*
experience, then you can't have experience-based values!

That is, you can't have as your goal to promote more pleasant experiences
and fewer painful experiences, either for yourself, for humanity, or
whatever.  All possible experiences will happen!  If you make a good choice
now, maybe more things in more universes will be associated with a
particular experience, but if there is no point to such extra "replays" of
an experience, there is no point to this difference.

Steve Witham writes:
>I think a chunk of history that's simply an interpolation between two
>others doesn't have an experience of its own because its evolution
>doesn't follow from its own state.

Michael Clive Price writes:
>If the interpolation is of trivial complexity (eg a simple polynomial
>between look-up values) then I would say the interpolation rule has no
>experiences.  If interpolation was very complex then the experiences are
>distributed across both the computation associated with the look-up table
>formation and the interpolation rule formation.

I don't want to say these are wrong, but just that there is lots of work to
be done to make such answers precise.  For Steve, what about
non-deterministic physics?  For Mike, just how complex does it take?  My
basic point was just that there is much we don't about this stuff.

Robin Hanson

