From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Mon, 22 Aug 1994 16:52:27 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-8-248] Smart Contracts (fwd)

Vince's reply to my essay was originally posted to exi-bay 
(I responded here by mistake); here it is forwarded with
permission:

--------
This is in response to Nick Szabo's post to the essay list about "Smart
Contracts".

I liked Nick's ideas very much, and think that we can implement many of
them with today's technology --for example, with software run on a laptop
or handheld computer.  Even very simple "smart" or computerized contracts
would be immediately beneficial to the high-tech (and especially the
software) industry for a number of reasons:

1.  To standardize contracts and negotiation processes across a whole
industry.  Currently, every firm uses its own custom set of contracts, each
with a different set of clauses and wordings, although most of them
(should) say the same things.

2.  To verify "good" and "legal" contracts.  Problems occur when
non-standard clauses are used, or when clauses which are not legally
enforceable in a given state (such as non-competition in CA) are
introduced.  Having a standard contract template for a given industry in a
given state, as well as an on-line legal database, would improve things
greatly, as well as reduce the risk of fraud.

3.  To encode PPLs, personal violation penalties, and escrows.  To a great
extent, contracts are becomming less and less worth the paper they're
printed on (e.g. "shrink-wrap" software licenses).  However, contracts are
a very good means of bookkeeping and recording information for private
non-litigation use.  PPL clauses between the parties involved (and not
enforceable in a public court) can be recorded, as well as the agreed-upon
penalties for their violation.  Escrows of money or information, along with
the associated RSA keys, can be held by a 3rd-party in the event of
violation.

4.  To verify party reputations and histories.  Before entering into a
contract, each party could check the reputation and payment history of the
other by use of a public-access RSA key and digital signature verification.
Successful conclusion (or violation) of a contract would automatically
update these files.

5.  To encode transaction promises, account info, and payment schedules.
Each milestone of a contracted project, as well as payment types, amounts,
and associated accounts and keys, could be encoded into the contract for
automatic execution at specified intervals.  This would eliminate the major
hassle which currently exists with "net-30", physical checks, and the like.

6.  To encode and transfer partial product "unlocking" passwords.  In
exchange for payment, contractees could automatically transfer to employers
a series of passwords or keys which enable or decode the work for each
milestone.  Currently, this would only be possible for work consisting of
software, firmware, or pure information.

7.  To automatically achieve 3rd-party archiving and contract copyrighting.
Strictly speaking, every contract should be archived with a 3rd-party,
often the US PTO (Patent & Trademark Office), since, in effect, every
contract is a unique configuration of symbols which may contain infringable
proprietary trade information.  Contracts usually specify assignment of
intellectual property and non-disclosure agreements, which must also be
tracked well beyond the lifetime of the original contract.

All of the above could be done with current software, and such a system
would certainly make my life easier.  If anyone has an interest in pursuing
development of a product along these lines, let me know.

--Vince


