Date: Tue, 23 Aug 1994 01:07:13 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-250] SOC/PSYCH External Causes

Here's a clip from Vince that pretty much raises all this issues in this
discussion so far:

>>While I thoroughly agree with Max that there are many transformations which
>>must inherently take place both personally and socially before the world
>>can be restructured more to my liking, I do not see this as an altruistic
>>task on my part.

(Maybe Max didn't either.)

>>However, whereas I am somewhat willing to spend at least
>>some of my own time and effort toward tasks which may not be directly
>>related to my own immediate self-interest (such as writing this reply), I
>>do so primarily because I have identified these tasks as being likely to
>>effect my well-being via the restructuring of my environment through
>>intermediate agents (such as yourselves).

I think there's a whole angle that this thread is missing so far, more
related to Max's review of "Good Mood:"  That there are personal,
psychological benefits to "doing things for others" that have a looser
action-to-benefit coupling than what Vince is talking about.  In fact it's
often readjusted benefit expectations and readjusted approaches to action
that are the benefits.

This is claimed by many religions and "spiritual traditions," and self-
promotion is an obvious explanation for that, but I've seen enough people say,
        "Be selfish
        be skeptical
        rid your mind of social conditioning
        don't be swayed by superficially positive or negative events
        focus on your goal
        and do things for others because it will get you there."
that I'm starting to suspect there's some truth to it.  My model of
religion is that it gets is power by getting *close* to important truths.

If nobody can imagine plausible spiritual benefits I'll make up some.

There's another angle: that if we are defined by our goals more than our
skins or our bank accounts, then maybe "doing for others" is just a clumsy
way of describing a meme-plex inhabiting a little more than one body.

 --Steve
